#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186293890 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b4df33ff-e04e-55ec-b273-9eceff7db53b" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Rotation Matrices"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1f73592d-1a3a-58aa-be0b-c2bf22972c9f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14033087/assets/489bc530fc35e2e740038264f2be05bffb90214249ec5602591623d457325db3.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293892} {:answer/id #uuid "b5653e6b-7125-50a8-9ac0-67659ec5449a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14033087/assets/ba328088ca187ea28b241a302a486da1145a51c7e134d8d4f529f5f4a916ddc1.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293893} {:answer/id #uuid "65a3beed-47ed-544c-80fd-8e3487b18804" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14033087/assets/5bd4d8cc2643767b82acc0e61d168f5b387be67a7ff96634604e0d6828cc288b.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293894} {:answer/id #uuid "daa88a91-2d37-5256-bdd5-2a7499c9b507" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14033087/assets/efb79fc1071f79597c81a0a974e7f6b21d361ac046f728625e2fe1c02bb507eb.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293895} {:answer/id #uuid "2acb9fbd-6a13-5bc4-aa20-d80083518450" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14033087/assets/2da0ffb873eb230718aab29c7dd09e015a0801b7ecb3b7dea91102f30b3e8816.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293896}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b5653e6b-7125-50a8-9ac0-67659ec5449a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14033087/assets/ba328088ca187ea28b241a302a486da1145a51c7e134d8d4f529f5f4a916ddc1.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293893} :answer-field/id #uuid "7c79d7c0-dfcf-5cba-beb2-f25c807802d7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293891}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "75802105-915e-5223-a4a4-b4c2831bb162" :question/math-academy-id "q-160619" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14033087/assets/f3e31d1c15a97adea32790eafe75ad1bc9f70c854b516b98de6648ec8d7bcb87.png)\n\nConsider the vector $v$ shown above. Find the vector $Av$ given that \n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}].\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Consider the standard basis ${i,j}$ of the Euclidean plane ${R}^{2}$ and the unit square spanned by the vectors. Computing the images of ${i,j}$ under $A$, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{i}^{′} & =A(i)=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]=-i \\\\ {j}^{′} & =A(j)=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]=-j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs a result, we obtain the following diagram:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14033087/assets/9fae3ec0ada3008937d88e4e76374a56da4369b1821cfee072c90d88b562df46.png)\n\nSo, the matrix represents a rotation of $\\pi $ radians (counterclockwise or clockwise) about the origin.\n\nTherefore, in order to find $Av$, we need to rotate $v$ (counterclockwise or clockwise) by an angle of $\\pi $ radians, as shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14033087/assets/ae800b271848948b5f60f5cd7c1cecd163c2aa7399e9a87e2a52d158857c6a42.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186293897 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b4df33ff-e04e-55ec-b273-9eceff7db53b" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Rotation Matrices"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a451e005-9920-55ba-aba2-4a82df7fdc89" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14033087/assets/4a78a3e98da4c602b4e8a2ebbe8860f75e507ec2ec5c20df0e59100f96a099ac.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293899} {:answer/id #uuid "12c5a080-b22a-52c1-9b3c-1e3a4833e96d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14033087/assets/cf86cad03c80018ac3976a64780650c827d7c93a0889286c711c5aa69d2b899f.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293900} {:answer/id #uuid "1bef1177-6e7a-54d6-8454-e86c89efdb92" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14033087/assets/f2beba76039ead47c83c93aaad5f9c4717a4abd2b803e65157f23d770f4f9181.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293901} {:answer/id #uuid "4504772e-8da4-5cff-a3c3-c6172ca608b2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14033087/assets/3edafbd6273f8058114c606c8b2a288e52b348d560867b6ea90e8b981b486904.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293902} {:answer/id #uuid "5e6b05d3-925f-5dad-adbd-f539eb186ef1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14033087/assets/82091407834f5d7aa8caf6e01cb800a3f7b1999cb0c6e568c899d43479b1d7a3.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293903}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a451e005-9920-55ba-aba2-4a82df7fdc89" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14033087/assets/4a78a3e98da4c602b4e8a2ebbe8860f75e507ec2ec5c20df0e59100f96a099ac.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293899} :answer-field/id #uuid "2c52ba30-782e-5f65-9f49-6868e4cb05f7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293898}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "efaa3158-b486-5ce8-9ecd-d7b2e13036d3" :question/math-academy-id "q-160621" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14033087/assets/b75a7519c7953b7a3c5bd5b5e61e961ca26c3f0dc9e1aab32bf2c639dd95325f.png)\n\nConsider the vector $v$ shown above. Find the vector $Av$ given that \n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Consider the standard basis ${i,j}$ of the Euclidean plane ${R}^{2}$ and the unit square spanned by the vectors. Computing the images of ${i,j}$ under $A$, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{i}^{′} & =Ai=[\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]=j \\\\ {j}^{′} & =Aj=[\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]=-i\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs a result, we obtain the following diagram:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14033087/assets/f154267ed0a528b1e9fbec40064b090560410fe76dab00070e13fb3d1978b59c.png)\n\nSo, the matrix represents a rotation of $\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ radians counterclockwise about the origin.\n\nTherefore, in order to find $Av$, we need to rotate $v$ counterclockwise by an angle of $\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ radians, as shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14033087/assets/8ea257c65769c4d6f67f55ce6533c1bfdfe45debcf1ba532b9ec3f717238507a.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186293904 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b4df33ff-e04e-55ec-b273-9eceff7db53b" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Rotation Matrices"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "49be66f3-ba66-5919-b1db-61bdeb019eba" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14033087/assets/70dfc0c8412bfae3bdbb7128fc021199f11d63d6f4ee264bea20da70dd18316c.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293906} {:answer/id #uuid "9a7d6100-079c-5012-9b5f-037a5687e6cb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14033087/assets/09b2ae9333118de0621a5f29610b6a83689fadaeb9c05e7a30b2799b12a1bd58.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293907} {:answer/id #uuid "708ee9ac-745d-55db-b410-f1f4aab15194" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14033087/assets/ead70b80d9256e1211addc404613f2dfe4a19baf822cbd77f429572ffc9a3a6d.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293908} {:answer/id #uuid "3c8adda5-0333-5dd2-8fb3-c3aec8bca265" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14033087/assets/5a1923eec2b66ef4eb861ec42b00ea48a3285aaaf3255c42d12c001c9cde32f8.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293909} {:answer/id #uuid "5bbcd6fb-ec6e-579f-beca-1eaad44b5f89" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14033087/assets/23f63717c8d356c698137359824112511b0bbd5ec04c59e47d22500d842d03b6.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293910}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "49be66f3-ba66-5919-b1db-61bdeb019eba" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14033087/assets/70dfc0c8412bfae3bdbb7128fc021199f11d63d6f4ee264bea20da70dd18316c.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293906} :answer-field/id #uuid "7099fd12-65a9-5316-9ba3-b4fd5bb3e69a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293905}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "04e79ef7-7090-527f-95f0-0f13ae5b3650" :question/math-academy-id "q-160627" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14033087/assets/0bd98626bc49cc3c58e503d5a7373b6af0fcce721cfdf82ff372eaa57d157dda.png)\n\nConsider the vector $v$ shown above. Find the vector $Av$ given that \n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Consider the standard basis ${i,j}$ of the Euclidean plane ${R}^{2}$ and the unit square spanned by the vectors. Computing the images of ${i,j}$ under $A$, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{i}^{′} & =Ai=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]=-j \\\\ {j}^{′} & =Aj=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]=i\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs a result, we obtain the following diagram:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14033087/assets/77936c4115b37d79ae301a93b218ecd23114ae4ad42d8298b8f058c1f50e82a2.png)\n\nSo, the matrix represents a rotation of $\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ radians **clockwise** about the origin.\n\nTherefore, in order to find $Av$, we need to rotate $v$ **clockwise** by an angle of $\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ radians, as shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14033087/assets/d5ff1c1a7c99b08d04d2ac58d9f8195ee29c8b564873a9abfb2635e93cec1968.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186293911 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "1b86558a-4c99-5ccb-a100-719692a056f4" :knowledge-point/title "Using a Matrix That Represents a Rotation of 90 Degrees"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "61129a8e-e817-555a-b8cc-c20f7bd58800" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293913} {:answer/id #uuid "ad5b0ee2-7af1-5000-b95c-9787ca1087c5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293914} {:answer/id #uuid "317cb7f7-cd1c-5803-88fa-e4caf85faf2a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293915} {:answer/id #uuid "b5f75dfa-b784-5059-a491-313dfb65c162" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293916} {:answer/id #uuid "bf7197e7-af48-5688-acbe-4eaf01deb432" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293917}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "61129a8e-e817-555a-b8cc-c20f7bd58800" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293913} :answer-field/id #uuid "0e1d7e5c-861f-50ad-96f8-5c1feba4e907" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293912}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "8d8905ff-87fc-56b7-a3d8-d73cbc677520" :question/math-academy-id "q-160644" :question/problem "A triangle $S$ has vertices at $(2,-3),(3,1),(-4,-1).$ Which of the following are coordinates of the vertices of ${S}^{′},$ the image of $S$ under the counterclockwise rotation ${R}_{{270}^{∘}}$ by an angle of ${270}^{∘}$ about the origin?\n\n- $(3,2)$\n- $(1,-3)$\n- $(-1,4)$" :question/worked-solution "The counterclockwise rotation ${R}_{{270}^{∘}}$ by an angle of ${270}^{∘}$ is given by the matrix\n\n$$\nR=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTo find the image of $S$ under the action of ${R}_{{270}^{∘}},$ we first create a matrix $X$ containing all of the vertices of $S:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}X & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 & -4 \\\\ -3 & 1 & -1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the image of $X$ under ${R}_{{270}^{∘}}$ by calculating the matrix product $RX:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}RX & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 & -4 \\\\ -3 & 1 & -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 1 & -1 \\\\ -2 & -3 & 4\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the respective vertices of ${S}^{′}$ are $(-3,-2),(1,-3),(-1,4).$\n\nSo, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186293918 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "1b86558a-4c99-5ccb-a100-719692a056f4" :knowledge-point/title "Using a Matrix That Represents a Rotation of 90 Degrees"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "984ee96b-4ac0-5e7a-bde6-7ed49bdf48de" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293920} {:answer/id #uuid "7f334c66-fa9a-5e90-a105-62c70d891948" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293921} {:answer/id #uuid "adc491b8-03b6-5657-b74f-02b5c0531bd7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293922} {:answer/id #uuid "522176c9-f8b4-52b4-a331-8bac2b70a135" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293923} {:answer/id #uuid "f4349533-283e-5819-88cb-0cd11c85c3df" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293924}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "522176c9-f8b4-52b4-a331-8bac2b70a135" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293923} :answer-field/id #uuid "039eab0d-68a9-546f-ae81-a9862d41ad0e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293919}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "8b8e4100-9820-5dcf-ac19-03a2a5373bbb" :question/math-academy-id "q-8427" :question/problem "A triangle $S$ has vertices at $(0,2),(-1,2),(-1,0).$ Which of the following are coordinates of the vertices of ${S}^{′},$ the image of $S$ under the counterclockwise rotation ${R}_{{90}^{∘}}$ by an angle of ${90}^{∘}$ about the origin?\n\n- $(1,-1)$\n- $(0,-2)$\n- $(-2,-1)$" :question/worked-solution "The counterclockwise rotation ${R}_{{90}^{∘}}$ by an angle of ${90}^{∘}$ is given by the matrix\n\n$$\nR=[\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTo find the image of $S$ under the action of ${R}_{{90}^{∘}},$ we first create a matrix $X$ containing all of the vertices of $S:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}X & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 2 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the image of $X$ under ${R}_{{90}^{∘}}$ by calculating the matrix product $RX:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}RX & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 2 & 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1 & -1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the respective vertices of ${S}^{′}$ are $(-2,0),(-2,-1),(0,-1).$\n\nSo, the correct answer is \"III only.\""}]}
